  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nToday:\n\nAn upper level extratropical cyclone that made landfall along the \nPacific Northwest Saturday morning on satellite water vapor imagery \nloops has slowly been shifting east over the last 24 hours driving a \nlarge area of confluence from the Klamath Mountains in northern \nCalifornia to the Coconino Plateau in Arizona. The resultant uptick \nin mid and upper level winds on GOES derived motion winds is \ninitially advecting tropical moisture through gaps in the Desert \nSouthwest towards the Northern Plains with 400 kg m-1 s-1 IVT racing \nnorth through Wyoming overnight. Supplementary, stronger low level \nmoisture advection, evident in 90th to daily record PWATs (SPC \nClimatology) at GJT and RIW in 14.00Z RAOBs combines with a directly \nTropical sourced airmass through the early morning, resulting in a \n1000+ km plume of 1000-1500 kg m-1 s-1 Integrated Vapor Transport \nfrom northern Kansas to the Canadian border with Minnesota. \n\nOngoing confluence of the aforementioned airmasses eventually causes \nPWATs near 2\" (EPS/GEFS) near the main directionally convergent \nboundary, near and southwest of the local forecast area tonight into \nearly Tuesday morning.\n\nRain Today:\n\nInitial rainfall expected with warm/moist air advection through the \nafternoon moving from southwest to northeast. Local rainfall rates \nexpected to remain mostly subdued as the nose of the low level jet \nwill be focused north towards the triple point of the deepening, \nclosing, and phasing low pressure. Supplementary cyclogenesis near \nthe forecast area will re-enhance local heavy rain potential in \nspots late Tuesday evening before a northwest to southeast advecting \nfrontal boundary sags southeast overnight into Tuesday morning. Deep \nwarm cloud depths from the surface to 600mb raise heavy rain and \nflooding concerns where strongest storms form and linger over the \nforecast area. Current confidence for a widespread 1\" of rainfall \nwith 2\"+ possible in spots where heaviest rain sets up tonight into \nearly Tuesday morning. While highest confidence for 1\"+/hr rainfall \nrates remain southwest of the local forecast area in central Iowa, \nmultiple ingredients support nearing those values in northeast Iowa.\n\nRain Returns Wednesday:\n\nThe warm, moist airmass remains just south of the local forecast \narea as passing anticyclonic low level flow mostly drying things out \nfor Tuesday before a similar trajectory ejecting upper level low \nlifts northeast through the Northern Plains for Wednesday. While the \nanomalous moisture will remain on the southern doorstep of the local \nforecast area, meridional extent of the low's path leaves \ndiscrepancies and lower confidence for northern surge of moist air. \nTherefore, have kept NBM precipitation probabilities Wednesday \nthrough Saturday. The long duration of rainfall potential would \nincrease additional flooding concerns through the week.\n\nStorm Chances Friday:\n\nThe warm, moist airmass will be lingering over the forecast area \nthrough late week when a Rocky Mountain Low is expected to eject \nnorth into the Northern Plains. Subsequent easterly advection along \nthe lingering low level boundary raises strong to severe storm \nconcerns across the Upper Midwest.",
